  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia helped to strengthen the Pacific&#x2019;s vector surveillance and control workforce through the PacMOSSI program (Pacific Mosquito Surveillance Strengthening for Impact), implemented by James Cook University (JCU).
&#x200d;
In November 2024, JCU held a practical training in Fiji focused on insecticide resistance monitoring of Aedes vector mosquitoes, to support PICs to generate essential local data to inform selection of effective vector control interventions. 14 participants from 11 PICs attended (64% female). JCU supplied participants with equipment to utilise in their home countries after the training, to enable application of the skills learnt for continued resistance monitoring.
&#x200d;
JCU also provided in-country technical support and training to vector officers in Samoa and Kiribati, in response to requests from the respective ministries of health during dengue outbreaks. Training topics included refresher mosquito surveillance and control, and outbreak preparedness and response, including specific training on residual spraying of insecticides during arboviral disease outbreaks.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported Pacific countries to better detect and control vector-borne disease threats through the PacMOSSI program (Pacific Mosquito Surveillance Strengthening for Impact), implemented by James Cook University (JCU). In 2024 JCU supported 19 PICs to complete Vector Control Needs Assessments (building on 2021 baseline assessments completed in Phase 1), detailing current activities and mosquito control capacity. JCU used these assessments to support Fiji and Tonga to finalise national vector control strategic plans. Support for Kiribati, Nauru and Niue is ongoing. JCU also supported the expansion of digital vector surveillance through the Tupaia data platform in 6 PICs, via collaboration with Beyond Essential Systems (BES). Finally, JCU delivered vector surveillance and control trainings, in-country technical trainings, and provided equipment to PICs.
&#x200d;
Australia supported dengue prevention through a partnership with the World Mosquito Program (WMP). In 2024, WMP began preparations for deploying the evidence-based Wolbachia intervention in Timor-Leste and Kiribati, as well as ongoing technical support for the Indonesian Government&#x2019;s Wolbachia project covering five cities with a high dengue burden. The intervention is a proven, self-sustaining, and cost-effective approach to protecting communities, through releasing mosquitos infected with Wolbachia bacteria, making them less capable of transmitting viruses such as dengue, Zika and chikungunya.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
